{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0119iiTdCMWaczsW9iWKnWRr","type":"tool_result","content":"    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"maximum hitting time for random walks on graphs lollipop extremal\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Lollipop/barbell are test families and the expected worst case for cheap-statistic predictors; extremal-graph theory explains family-dependent error.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"cover time of random regular graphs and sparse Erdős–Rényi random graphs\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Random regular and Erdős–Rényi are benchmark families; asymptotic cover times give context for where simple predictors should be reliable.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"ridge regression biased estimation for nonorthogonal problems\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The evaluated baseline is ridge regression on standardized log features.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"generic 'optimization', 'benchmarking', 'scientific method', or paper-writing phrases\",\n    \"queries about run chronology, attempts, or search history\",\n    \"queries targeting the original paper answering this research question or any page discussing it (task prohibits)\",\n    \"deep-learning/GNN cover-time prediction queries not supported by artifacts\",\n    \"external dataset names (none used)\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}@Article{Feige1995ATL,\n author = {U. Feige},\n booktitle = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n journal = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n pages = {433-438},\n title = {A Tight Lower Bound on the Cover Time for Random Walks on Graphs},\n volume = {6},\n year = {1995}\n}\n\n@Article{Feige1995ATU,\n author = {U. Feige},\n booktitle = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n journal = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n pages = {51-54},\n title = {A Tight Upper Bound on the Cover Time for Random Walks on Graphs},\n volume = {6},\n year = {1995}\n}\n\n@Article{Kiwi2022CoverAH,\n author = {M. Kiwi and Markus Schepers and John A. Sylvester},\n booktitle = {International Workshop and International Workshop on Approximation, Randomization, and Combinatorial Optimization. Algorithms and Techniques},\n journal = {Random Structures & Algorithms},\n pages = {915 - 978},\n title = {Cover and hitting times of hyperbolic random graphs},\n volume = {65},\n year = {2022}\n}\n35:Worst-case bounds on the cover time of a simple random walk, such as $2m(n-1)$, are known to be loose for concrete small graphs. We ask whether five cheap, relabeling-invariant statistics (vertices, edges, mean degree, maximum degree, diameter) are enough to predict Monte Carlo start-averaged cover time more accurately than this bound on connected graphs with $6\\le n\\le 30$. We study a ridge regression from log-statistics to log cover time, fitted in-episode on 36 labelled synthetic graphs from 12 families and scored on 24 disjoint held-out graphs in a sealed evaluation. Its family-macro log mean absolute error is $0.181$, against $1.754$ for the bound, $0.870$ for a constant predictor, and $1.423$ when the same predictions are scored against shuffled targets; the median relative Monte Carlo standard error of the labels is $0.029$. The sealed evaluation completed with a qualified outcome. The evidence is narrow: two test graphs per family, a deterministic predictor whose three seeded episodes coincide, no uncertainty over graph draws, and no retained per-family errors, so the question of \\emph{which graph types} the predictor fails on is left unanswered. The result characterises this sample of synthetic families and sizes; it does not establish reliability beyond them.\n39:The cover time of a connected undirected graph is the expected number of steps a simple random walk needs to visit every vertex. Classical analysis gives bounds in terms of the number of vertices $n$ and edges $m$: Aleliunas et al.\\ showed that the cover time from any start is at most $2m(n-1)$, and Feige proved that no connected graph exceeds $(4/27+o(1))n^3$ and none falls below $(1+o(1))\\,n\\ln n$ \\citep{Feige1995ATU,Feige1995ATL}. These statements are tight for extremal graphs, but they say little about what to expect for a particular small graph: the bound $2m(n-1)$ is attained up to constant factors only by bottlenecked graphs such as paths and lollipops, and on dense or well-connected graphs it overshoots by a factor that grows with $n$ (Section~\\ref{sec:rationale}).\n43:\\paragraph{What is studied.} The object is a deliberately simple reference predictor: standardised log-statistics, ridge regression, and a power-law reading of the result, $\\widehat{C}\\propto \\prod_j s_j^{w_j}$. We claim no novelty for the model class. The contribution is a careful measurement: a sealed, content-disjoint evaluation with a pre-declared aggregation (family-macro mean of clipped log error), three baselines or controls scored under the same pipeline, a Monte Carlo noise-floor estimate for the labels, and an explicit accounting of what the evidence cannot support.\n47:\\item A precisely specified five-statistic log-linear ridge predictor for start-averaged cover time on connected graphs with $6\\le n\\le 30$, with an observation that three of its five features are exactly collinear, so the ridge penalty is structurally necessary (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n55:\\caption{Overview of the study. A connected graph ($6\\le n\\le 30$) is reduced to five relabeling-invariant statistics; a ridge model predicts log cover time, which is compared with a Monte Carlo label. The $2m(n-1)$ bound, a constant predictor and a shuffled-target control are scored through the same pipeline: per-graph clipped log error $\\rightarrow$ family mean $\\rightarrow$ family-macro mean $\\rightarrow$ mean over seeded episodes.}\n60:\\paragraph{Worst-case bounds.} The $2m(n-1)$ bound of Aleliunas et al.\\ follows by summing commute times, each at most $2m$, over the $n-1$ edges of a spanning tree. Feige's upper bound, $(4/27+o(1))n^3$, is attained by lollipop-like graphs, and his companion lower bound, $(1+o(1))\\,n\\ln n$, is attained by the complete graph \\citep{Feige1995ATU,Feige1995ATL}. Together these delimit the possible range of cover times for a given $n$ but are worst-case over all connected graphs; they are not intended as point predictions for a given graph. Lov\\'asz's survey on random walks on graphs collects the hitting-time and commute-time machinery behind such bounds. Our comparison against $2m(n-1)$ therefore measures how much a worst-case guarantee costs as a predictor on small graphs; it is not a criticism of the bound, which remains valid.\n62:\\paragraph{Cover times of specific models.} A complementary line of work derives the cover and hitting times of particular random-graph models, for example hyperbolic random graphs \\citep{Kiwi2022CoverAH}. Such results exploit the generative structure of one model and give asymptotic statements. Our setting is different in three ways: the graphs form a mixture of twelve deterministic and random families, they are small, and the predictor is not told the family, so it must rely on statistics that are cheap to compute for any input graph.\n89:\\paragraph{Five-statistic ridge predictor (studied).} For a graph with maximum degree $\\Delta$, diameter $D$ (at least $1$) and mean degree $\\bar d=2m/n$, the feature vector is\n97:with the intercept $\\bar y$ unpenalised, and the prediction is $\\hat y(G)=\\exp\\!\\big(\\bar y+z(G)^\\top w\\big)$ for the standardised feature $z(G)$. Because the model is linear in log-features it is a power law, $\\hat y\\propto n^{a_1}m^{a_2}\\bar d^{a_3}\\Delta^{a_4}D^{a_5}$. Since $\\bar d=2m/n$, we have $\\ln m=\\ln n+\\ln\\bar d-\\ln 2$ exactly, so the three features are linearly dependent and the design matrix has rank at most four; the ridge term is what makes the normal equations well posed and selects the minimum-norm solution in standardised coordinates. The model is refitted on all accumulated labelled graphs after each batch of training graphs. If no training graphs have been supplied, the implementation falls back to the bound itself; this safeguard is not part of the comparison.\n101:\\paragraph{Constant baseline.} The prediction is the geometric mean of the training Monte Carlo targets, $\\exp(\\bar y)$, for every test graph. This is exactly the ridge model with all weights set to zero, so the ridge-versus-constant comparison is a controlled ablation of the entire feature set under an identical intercept.\n103:\\paragraph{Shuffled-target control.} The ridge predictions are scored against test targets permuted across graphs by a fixed derangement. A predictor that tracked the targets because of an artefact of the metric, rather than of graph structure, would score similarly under this control; the control therefore calibrates how large the metric is when predictions carry no information about the matched labels.\n107:\\paragraph{Effective parameterisation.} Because $\\ln m=\\ln n+\\ln\\bar d-\\ln 2$, the five-feature power law is equivalent to a four-parameter family in $(\\ln n,\\ln \\bar d,\\ln\\Delta,\\ln D)$; the ridge solution distributes the shared signal over the three collinear features in the minimum-norm way. Individual fitted weights on $n$, $m$ and $\\bar d$ are therefore not separately interpretable, and we do not interpret them. Only the fitted function (the prediction) is meaningful.\n125:The predictor is a self-contained Python module of about $140$ lines implementing the reset/fit/predict interface using only the standard library: breadth-first search from every vertex gives the diameter; a small Gaussian elimination with partial pivoting solves the five-by-five ridge system. Predictions are clipped from below at $10^{-9}$ to remain positive. The only hyper-parameter is $\\lambda=1$, a fixed constant in the source that was not tuned on the sealed graphs. Source locations are listed in the Supplementary Information.\n131:Table~\\ref{tab:main} and Figure~\\ref{fig:main} report the sealed-evaluation scores. The five-statistic ridge predictor attains a family-macro log MAE of $0.181$ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.macro_log_mae]]\n136:so the ridge error is about one fifth of the constant error and about one tenth of the bound error (ratios $4.8$ and $9.7$; arithmetic on the reported values).\n141:\\caption{Family-macro log MAE on the sealed held-out graphs (lower is better). The ridge predictor is compared with the $2m(n-1)$ bound, a constant predictor equal to the training geometric mean, and the ridge predictions scored against shuffled targets. Each bar is one evaluator-computed value; no error bars are shown because no uncertainty over graph draws was computed.}\n147:\\caption{Sealed-evaluation results. ``Factor'' is $\\exp$ of the family-macro log MAE and ``Ratio'' is the log MAE divided by that of the ridge predictor; both are arithmetic conversions of the reported values.}\n154:Five-statistic ridge & $0.181$ & $1.20$ & $1.0$ \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.macro_log_mae]]\n163:\\paragraph{Constant control as feature ablation.} Because the constant predictor shares the ridge intercept, the drop from $0.870$ to $0.181$ is attributable, within this evaluation, to the five-statistic term as a whole. The evaluation does \\emph{not} contain per-feature ablations, so it cannot say which statistics matter, whether the collinear triple $(n,m,\\bar d)$ contributes beyond two of its members, or whether the diameter feature carries the gain. Those attributions would require experiments that were not performed.\n165:\\paragraph{Shuffled-target control.} Scoring the same predictions against permuted targets yields $1.423$, much larger than $0.181$ and comparable in magnitude to the bound's $1.754$. The low error of the ridge predictor is therefore not a property of the metric or of the label distribution alone: when predictions are decoupled from their graphs' labels, the metric is roughly eight times larger. That the shuffled control is worse than the constant predictor is unsurprising: a constant hedges near the centre of the log-label distribution, whereas permuted predictions keep the full spread of the ridge outputs and pair it with the wrong labels.\n172:about $2.9\\%$, or roughly one sixth of the ridge log MAE ($0.029/0.181\\approx0.16$; arithmetic). The label noise is thus small relative to the prediction error and very small relative to the bound's error, and the headline gap cannot be explained by label noise. A simple calibration helps: if the predictor were exact and each label were perturbed by a Gaussian relative error with standard deviation equal to this median, the expected absolute log error would be $\\sqrt{2/\\pi}\\times0.029\\approx0.023$, about $13\\%$ of the observed $0.181$. % @evidence[[closed_form_gap.json#noise_floor_mae_gaussian]]\n173:This is an order-of-magnitude statement under a Gaussian approximation, not a measured noise floor, but it indicates that label noise explains only a minor part of the ridge error. Two cautions apply. First, this is a median: the retained record does not include the maximum standard error or the per-graph values, so the noisiest graphs (likely the slowest-covering families) may carry larger relative uncertainty. Second, the standard error treats the walks as independent samples of cover time and characterises only simulation noise; it does not include the variability of which graphs were drawn.\n195:\\paragraph{Training-set size.} The model is fitted on $36$ labelled graphs (three per family) with five features of rank four; the ridge penalty $\\lambda=1$ is a fixed constant. Whether the result is sensitive to $\\lambda$, to training-set size or to the composition of the training families was not measured.\n197:\\paragraph{Feature attribution and ceiling.} We do not know which features carry the gain, nor how close a function of these five statistics can get to the label: structurally different graphs can share all five statistics. Richer invariants (for instance, resistance-based quantities motivated by the commute-time identity and Matthews-type bounds) are natural candidates, but a configuration of that kind produced no score in this study and we draw no conclusion about it.\n199:\\paragraph{Range of validity.} All graphs have $n\\le 30$ and are drawn from twelve named families; labels are start-averaged, not worst-case-start, cover times. Extrapolation to larger graphs, to other families (e.g.\\ scale-free or hyperbolic random graphs \\citep{Kiwi2022CoverAH}) or to the maximum over start vertices is untested. A power-law fit calibrated at $n\\le 30$ has no guarantee at larger $n$ and, in particular, should not be expected to respect the extremal exponents known from \\citet{Feige1995ATU}.\n227:For connected graphs with at most $30$ vertices, a ridge regression on five relabeling-invariant log-statistics predicted Monte Carlo start-averaged cover time with a family-macro log MAE of $0.181$ in a sealed evaluation, compared with $1.754$ for the generic $2m(n-1)$ bound, $0.870$ for a constant predictor, and $1.423$ when the predictions were scored against shuffled targets. Median relative Monte Carlo label error was $0.029$, small compared with the gap. The evaluation completed with a qualified outcome. In answer to the first research question, cheap statistics can give a markedly more accurate point prediction than the bound on this sample; in answer to the second, the retained evidence does not say on which graph types the predictor is reliable or fails. The most valuable next measurements are inexpensive and well-defined: per-family and per-size error reporting with Monte Carlo and graph-bootstrap uncertainty, per-feature ablations, sensitivity to the ridge penalty and training-set composition, and a comparison with richer invariants such as resistance-based features.\n244:\\caption{Implementation elements of the five-statistic ridge predictor.}\n308:The configuration studied here is the reference configuration retained by model selection. The selection procedure considered one alternative candidate, a resistance-calibrated Matthews-style predictor with a ridge residual, and it produced no score: zero candidates were scored and one was unscored. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.attempt_counts]]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fdbb2104-2c82-471e-ae57-fd7d726660d2","uuid":"4331d0c8-35db-463e-b30e-28f759e6725e","timestamp":"2026-10-27T17:48:14.528Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"maximum hitting time for random walks on graphs lollipop extremal\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Lollipop/barbell are test families and the expected worst case for cheap-statistic predictors; extremal-graph theory explains family-dependent error.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"cover time of random regular graphs and sparse Erdős–Rényi random graphs\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Random regular and Erdős–Rényi are benchmark families; asymptotic cover times give context for where simple predictors should be reliable.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"ridge regression biased estimation for nonorthogonal problems\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The evaluated baseline is ridge regression on standardized log features.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"generic 'optimization', 'benchmarking', 'scientific method', or paper-writing phrases\",\n    \"queries about run chronology, attempts, or search history\",\n    \"queries targeting the original paper answering this research question or any page discussing it (task prohibits)\",\n    \"deep-learning/GNN cover-time prediction queries not supported by artifacts\",\n    \"external dataset names (none used)\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}@Article{Feige1995ATL,\n author = {U. Feige},\n booktitle = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n journal = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n pages = {433-438},\n title = {A Tight Lower Bound on the Cover Time for Random Walks on Graphs},\n volume = {6},\n year = {1995}\n}\n\n@Article{Feige1995ATU,\n author = {U. Feige},\n booktitle = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n journal = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n pages = {51-54},\n title = {A Tight Upper Bound on the Cover Time for Random Walks on Graphs},\n volume = {6},\n year = {1995}\n}\n\n@Article{Kiwi2022CoverAH,\n author = {M. Kiwi and Markus Schepers and John A. Sylvester},\n booktitle = {International Workshop and International Workshop on Approximation, Randomization, and Combinatorial Optimization. Algorithms and Techniques},\n journal = {Random Structures & Algorithms},\n pages = {915 - 978},\n title = {Cover and hitting times of hyperbolic random graphs},\n volume = {65},\n year = {2022}\n}\n35:Worst-case bounds on the cover time of a simple random walk, such as $2m(n-1)$, are known to be loose for concrete small graphs. We ask whether five cheap, relabeling-invariant statistics (vertices, edges, mean degree, maximum degree, diameter) are enough to predict Monte Carlo start-averaged cover time more accurately than this bound on connected graphs with $6\\le n\\le 30$. We study a ridge regression from log-statistics to log cover time, fitted in-episode on 36 labelled synthetic graphs from 12 families and scored on 24 disjoint held-out graphs in a sealed evaluation. Its family-macro log mean absolute error is $0.181$, against $1.754$ for the bound, $0.870$ for a constant predictor, and $1.423$ when the same predictions are scored against shuffled targets; the median relative Monte Carlo standard error of the labels is $0.029$. The sealed evaluation completed with a qualified outcome. The evidence is narrow: two test graphs per family, a deterministic predictor whose three seeded episodes coincide, no uncertainty over graph draws, and no retained per-family errors, so the question of \\emph{which graph types} the predictor fails on is left unanswered. The result characterises this sample of synthetic families and sizes; it does not establish reliability beyond them.\n39:The cover time of a connected undirected graph is the expected number of steps a simple random walk needs to visit every vertex. Classical analysis gives bounds in terms of the number of vertices $n$ and edges $m$: Aleliunas et al.\\ showed that the cover time from any start is at most $2m(n-1)$, and Feige proved that no connected graph exceeds $(4/27+o(1))n^3$ and none falls below $(1+o(1))\\,n\\ln n$ \\citep{Feige1995ATU,Feige1995ATL}. These statements are tight for extremal graphs, but they say little about what to expect for a particular small graph: the bound $2m(n-1)$ is attained up to constant factors only by bottlenecked graphs such as paths and lollipops, and on dense or well-connected graphs it overshoots by a factor that grows with $n$ (Section~\\ref{sec:rationale}).\n43:\\paragraph{What is studied.} The object is a deliberately simple reference predictor: standardised log-statistics, ridge regression, and a power-law reading of the result, $\\widehat{C}\\propto \\prod_j s_j^{w_j}$. We claim no novelty for the model class. The contribution is a careful measurement: a sealed, content-disjoint evaluation with a pre-declared aggregation (family-macro mean of clipped log error), three baselines or controls scored under the same pipeline, a Monte Carlo noise-floor estimate for the labels, and an explicit accounting of what the evidence cannot support.\n47:\\item A precisely specified five-statistic log-linear ridge predictor for start-averaged cover time on connected graphs with $6\\le n\\le 30$, with an observation that three of its five features are exactly collinear, so the ridge penalty is structurally necessary (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n55:\\caption{Overview of the study. A connected graph ($6\\le n\\le 30$) is reduced to five relabeling-invariant statistics; a ridge model predicts log cover time, which is compared with a Monte Carlo label. The $2m(n-1)$ bound, a constant predictor and a shuffled-target control are scored through the same pipeline: per-graph clipped log error $\\rightarrow$ family mean $\\rightarrow$ family-macro mean $\\rightarrow$ mean over seeded episodes.}\n60:\\paragraph{Worst-case bounds.} The $2m(n-1)$ bound of Aleliunas et al.\\ follows by summing commute times, each at most $2m$, over the $n-1$ edges of a spanning tree. Feige's upper bound, $(4/27+o(1))n^3$, is attained by lollipop-like graphs, and his companion lower bound, $(1+o(1))\\,n\\ln n$, is attained by the complete graph \\citep{Feige1995ATU,Feige1995ATL}. Together these delimit the possible range of cover times for a given $n$ but are worst-case over all connected graphs; they are not intended as point predictions for a given graph. Lov\\'asz's survey on random walks on graphs collects the hitting-time and commute-time machinery behind such bounds. Our comparison against $2m(n-1)$ therefore measures how much a worst-case guarantee costs as a predictor on small graphs; it is not a criticism of the bound, which remains valid.\n62:\\paragraph{Cover times of specific models.} A complementary line of work derives the cover and hitting times of particular random-graph models, for example hyperbolic random graphs \\citep{Kiwi2022CoverAH}. Such results exploit the generative structure of one model and give asymptotic statements. Our setting is different in three ways: the graphs form a mixture of twelve deterministic and random families, they are small, and the predictor is not told the family, so it must rely on statistics that are cheap to compute for any input graph.\n89:\\paragraph{Five-statistic ridge predictor (studied).} For a graph with maximum degree $\\Delta$, diameter $D$ (